[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 2:13] Understood. You expect the price increase to recover 5.3 points of margin with volume held flat. What is the single biggest risk to that, and how would you know early whether it's materializing?

[candidate · 2:30] So, first, and this is important — the single biggest risk is volume loss, and more precisely it's that my "volume held flat" assumption is doing an enormous amount of work in that calculation. Let me size how much it can break. Margin today is 6%, so contribution per dollar of revenue after COGS is 100 minus 58, which is 42 cents. If I raise price 10%, revenue per unit goes to 110 and COGS per unit stays at 58, so contribution per unit goes to 52 cents. For the price increase to be margin-neutral in dollar terms, I'd need 52 times the new volume to equal 42 times the old volume, so new volume over old volume equals 42 over 52, about 0.81. So I can lose roughly 19% of units before the increase stops helping on contribution dollars. That's actually a fairly wide cushion, and it's why I'm comfortable leading with price — but it's a cushion, not a guarantee.

So, second, and this is how I'd detect it early, I'd watch transaction counts, not revenue. And I want to be emphatic about that, because revenue is exactly the metric that will lie to us here. If I raise price 10% and lose 8% of transactions, revenue barely moves and looks fine, while the underlying franchise is quietly eroding. So the early-warning metric is units — transactions per store per day, in the pilot markets, versus matched control markets that didn't take the increase. I'd read it weekly, and I'd expect a short-term dip in the first two to three weeks from sticker shock that partially recovers as customers re-anchor. So I'd want at least six to eight weeks before judging, and I'd specifically ask whether we have historical transaction volume data, because without a baseline I can't tell a price reaction from normal seasonality.

So, third, and this is the subtler version of the same risk, I'd watch ticket composition rather than just ticket count. Because customers often don't stop coming — they trade down. They move from a four-dollar latte to a two-dollar drip, or they drop the pastry. That shows up as stable transactions with falling average ticket, and it would partially or fully eat the 5.3 points without ever appearing as a traffic loss. So I'd track average ticket and item mix alongside counts.

Coming back to my first point on the 19% cushion — the reason I think we clear it is that coffee is habitual, the purchase is low absolute dollar value, and competitors faced identical commodity inflation over the same two years, so they have almost certainly already moved. If we're currently priced below market, we may have more headroom than 10%.

And returning to my second point on transactions versus revenue — the governance mechanism I'd put in place is a pre-committed rollback threshold. Something like: if transactions in pilot markets fall more than 10% versus control and haven't recovered by week six, we pause the national rollout and fall back to procurement and mix as the primary levers.

And circling back to my third point on trade-down — I'd also segment the elasticity read by daypart and by customer type, because the morning commuter is far less price-sensitive than the afternoon discretionary visit, and that would let us price asymmetrically rather than taking a flat 10% everywhere.
